.TH ANKER\-LAUNCHER 1 "2026-11-18" "anker-launcher" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
anker\-launcher \- run the AnkerGames launcher with WebKit GPU rendering enabled
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B anker\-launcher
[\fIURL\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B anker\-launcher
is a wrapper around the upstream AnkerGames desktop client installed at
.IR /opt/anker\-launcher\-bin/AppRun .
It exists for one reason: the client is a Tauri application using
.B WebKitGTK
4.1, and on startup it sets two WebKit environment variables to values that
force software rendering on every Linux system:
.IP \(bu 2
.B WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER=1
.IP \(bu 2
.B WEBKIT_SKIA_GPU_PAINTING_THREADS=0
.P
With those values, WebKit rasterises page content on the CPU and the GTK UI
process presents each frame through a freshly allocated
.B wl_shm
pool. The cost of a frame is then proportional to window area: at 1280x800 the
client keeps two processes busy repainting ~60 times a second even when idle,
and at 2560x1440 scrolling becomes unusable.
.P
The client only writes those variables when they are not already present, so a
wrapper is enough: this script exports GPU values and then
.BR exec s
the AppImage. Measured on an RX 9070 with Mesa 26, scrolling CPU in the UI
process falls from roughly 14% to 3% at 1280x800. No file inside
.B /opt
is modified.
.P
Any arguments are passed through to the AppImage unchanged, so URI activation
from the desktop entry
.RB ( x\-scheme\-handler=ankergames )
keeps working.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ANKER_SOFTWARE_RENDER
Set to
.B 1
to skip the overrides and launch the client exactly as upstream ships it, i.e.
with software rendering. Use this if a driver update makes the GPU path render
a blank or corrupt window.
.TP
.B WEBKIT_DISABLE_DMABUF_RENDERER
Pre-set by the wrapper to
.B 0
(dmabuf buffers shared with the compositor). A value supplied in the calling
environment is preserved.
.TP
.B WEBKIT_SKIA_GPU_PAINTING_THREADS
Pre-set by the wrapper to
.B 4
(GPU painting threads). A value supplied in the calling environment is
preserved. Setting it to
.B 0
reproduces the slow software path even when the dmabuf override is active.
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the launcher normally:
.IP
.EX
anker\-launcher
.EE
.P
Open the launcher on a store page:
.IP
.EX
anker\-launcher "ankergames://store"
.EE
.P
Reproduce upstream behaviour to compare, or to work around a broken GPU path:
.IP
.EX
ANKER_SOFTWARE_RENDER=1 anker\-launcher
.EE
.P
Use a different number of GPU painting threads:
.IP
.EX
WEBKIT_SKIA_GPU_PAINTING_THREADS=2 anker\-launcher
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The launcher exited normally.
.TP
.B other
Whatever the AppImage returned; the wrapper does not alter exit codes.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/anker\-launcher\-bin/AppRun
The upstream launcher that this script execs.
.TP
.I /usr/bin/anker\-launcher
Distribution launcher entry point, shadowed for interactive use by this script
when
.I ~/.local/bin
comes first in
.BR PATH .
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/applications/anker\-launcher.desktop
Desktop entry that points at this wrapper so menu launches get the GPU path.
.TP
.I ~/.config/net.ankergames.launcher/
Launcher preferences, library and session data.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dotfiles\-sync (1),
.BR webkit2gtk\-4.1 (3)
.SH BUGS
The override relies on the upstream client continuing to fill in the two
variables only when they are unset. A future version that assigns them
unconditionally will silently invalidate this wrapper.
.SH AUTHOR
Written for local use.
